Jac has a built-in filter comprehension on collections that makes this really clean — you can filter a list *by type* directly with `[?:TypeName]`. First let's define the two object types and a mixed list:

```jac
obj Dog { has name: str, age: int; }
obj Cat { has name: str, age: int; }

with entry {
    pets: list = [
        Dog(name="Rex", age=5),
        Cat(name="Whiskers", age=3),
        Dog(name="Buddy", age=2)
    ];

    # Keep only Dogs
    dogs = pets[?:Dog];
    print(dogs);
}
```

The `pets[?:Dog]` part keeps only the elements that are instances of `Dog`, so you'd get Rex and Buddy back.

Yes — the same `[? ... ]` filter takes a condition on the fields. Inside the brackets you just reference the field name directly, e.g. `pets[?age < 4]`:

```jac
obj Dog { has name: str, age: int; }
obj Cat { has name: str, age: int; }

with entry {
    pets: list = [
        Dog(name="Rex", age=5),
        Cat(name="Whiskers", age=3),
        Dog(name="Buddy", age=2)
    ];

    dogs = pets[?:Dog];
    print(dogs);

    # Filter by a field condition
    young = pets[?age < 4];
    print(young);
}
```

That gives you Whiskers and Buddy.

You can put both a type filter and a field condition in the same brackets, separated by a comma: `pets[?:Dog, age < 4]`. Here's the complete script with all three filters:

```jac
obj Dog { has name: str, age: int; }
obj Cat { has name: str, age: int; }

with entry {
    pets: list = [
        Dog(name="Rex", age=5),
        Cat(name="Whiskers", age=3),
        Dog(name="Buddy", age=2)
    ];

    # Filter by type -- keep only Dogs
    dogs = pets[?:Dog];
    print(dogs);  # [Dog(name='Rex', age=5), Dog(name='Buddy', age=2)]

    # Filter by field condition
    young = pets[?age < 4];
    print(young);  # [Cat(name='Whiskers', age=3), Dog(name='Buddy', age=2)]

    # Combined type + field filter
    young_dogs = pets[?:Dog, age < 4];
    print(young_dogs);  # [Dog(name='Buddy', age=2)]
}
```

The combined filter only keeps Buddy — the one element that is both a `Dog` and has `age < 4`.

