The narrowing dance in entry (isinstance dict, then isinstance str, then build a Task) is noisy. Pull it into a typed helper `to_task(raw)` that returns a `Task` or `None`, so entry is just parse -> to_task -> print. Code:

```jac
import json;

def parse(text: str) -> any {
    return json.loads(text);
}

obj Task { has title: str = ""; }

with entry {
    raw = parse('{"title": "ship"}');
    if isinstance(raw, dict) {
        title = raw.get("title", "");
        if isinstance(title, str) {
            t = Task(title=title);
            print(t.title);
        }
    }
}
```

Good -- moving the `any`-narrowing into one function with a real return type documents the intent. It returns `Task | None`; entry checks for `None` before printing. Here:

```jac
import json;

obj Task { has title: str = ""; }

def parse(text: str) -> any {
    return json.loads(text);
}

def to_task(raw: any) -> Task | None {
    if isinstance(raw, dict) {
        title = raw.get("title", "");
        if isinstance(title, str) {
            return Task(title=title);
        }
    }
    return None;
}

with entry {
    raw = parse('{"title": "ship"}');
    t = to_task(raw);
    if t is not None {
        print(t.title);
    }
}
```

Use an explicit `if t is not None`, which is what the code above does. Relying on truthiness (`if t`) would work here since a `Task` is truthy, but it's fragile if the object ever defines falsy semantics, and the explicit `is not None` matches the declared `Task | None` return type precisely. The refactor preserves behavior: valid input prints `ship`, malformed input yields `None` and prints nothing.

