I've got an auth walker guarding my endpoints and something is very wrong with it. Requests that come in with no token at all are coming back as authenticated, but the ones that actually send a proper `Bearer ...` header get slapped with a 401. It's completely backwards.

```jac
walker _authenticate {
    has headers: dict;

    can check with Root entry {
        token = self.headers.get("Authorization", "");

        if token.startswith("Bearer ") {
            report {"error": "Unauthorized", "status": 401};
            return;
        }

        # Validate token...
        report {"authenticated": True};
    }
}
```

Here's the walker. As far as I can tell anyone without a token sails right through, which is obviously a security hole.

Right, then it's the condition itself that's inverted. Read it literally: `if token.startswith("Bearer ")` means "IF this is a valid Bearer token, reject it with 401." That's exactly backwards. You want to reject the requests that are NOT Bearer tokens. Flip the test with `not`:

```jac
walker _authenticate {
    has headers: dict;

    can check with Root entry {
        token = self.headers.get("Authorization", "");

        if not token.startswith("Bearer ") {
            report {"error": "Unauthorized", "status": 401};
            return;
        }

        # Validate token...
        report {"authenticated": True};
    }
}
```

Now a missing/malformed token fails the `not ...` check and gets the 401, and a real Bearer token falls through to validation. That closes the bypass.

I have three walkers with different visibility levels and one of them is leaking data. `get_profile` is supposed to require a login, but anonymous callers are able to hit it and it dumps profile nodes straight out of the graph.

```jac
# Protected (default) -- requires JWT token
walker :pub get_profile {
    can fetch with Root entry { report [-->]; }
}

# Public -- no authentication required
walker :pub health_check {
    can check with Root entry { report {"status": "ok"}; }
}

# Private -- requires authentication, per-user isolated
walker :priv internal_process {
    can run with Root entry { }
}
```

This is the file. `health_check` being public is intended, but `get_profile` should NOT be reachable without a token.

You're right, don't hand-roll it. Look at the decorators: you wrote `walker :pub get_profile`. In Jac, `:pub` is exactly what makes a walker reachable with no JWT. The comment even says "Protected (default) -- requires JWT token", but the code marks it `:pub`, contradicting the comment. Protected is the *default*, so you get it by writing no visibility marker at all. Drop the `:pub`:

```jac
# Protected (default) -- requires JWT token
walker get_profile {
    can fetch with Root entry { report [-->]; }
}

# Public -- no authentication required
walker :pub health_check {
    can check with Root entry { report {"status": "ok"}; }
}

# Private -- requires authentication, per-user isolated
walker :priv internal_process {
    can run with Root entry { }
}
```

`health_check` stays `:pub` (genuinely public), `internal_process` stays `:priv`, and `get_profile` now falls back to the protected default, so it requires a token again.

My friend-of-friend traversal is skipping an entire layer of the graph. The person directly reachable from where I start gets labeled as a 'friend of friend' immediately and never gets expanded, so I lose everyone one hop deeper.

```jac
node Person {
    has name: str;

    can announce with FriendFinder entry {
        print(f"{visitor} is checking me out");
    }
}

edge Friend {}
edge Family {
    can announce with FriendFinder entry {
        print(f"{visitor} is traveling to family member");
    }
}

with entry {
    # Build the graph
    p1 = Person(name="John");
    p2 = Person(name="Susan");
    p3 = Person(name="Mike");
    p4 = Person(name="Alice");
    root ++> p1;
    p1 +>: Friend :+> p2;
    p2 +>: Family :+> [p1, p3];
    p2 +>: Friend :+> p3;
}

walker FriendFinder {
    has started: bool = True;

    can report_friend with Person entry {
        if self.started {
            print(f"{here.name} is a friend of friend, or family");
        } else {
            self.started = True;
            visit [-->];
        }
        visit [edge ->:Family :->];
    }

    can move_to_person with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }
}

with entry {
    result = FriendFinder() spawn root;
    print(result);
}
```

Here's the walker and the graph it runs on. The very first Person it lands on is treated as if the walk had already been going for a while.

Agreed, back that change out. The bug is the initial state of the flag. You have `has started: bool = True`, so on the *first* Person the walker reaches, `self.started` is already True, it takes the `if` branch (prints 'friend of friend') and never runs the `else` that would set the flag and do `visit [-->]`. The flag is meant to represent "have we moved past the starting point yet", which is False when the walk begins. Initialize it to False:

```jac
node Person {
    has name: str;

    can announce with FriendFinder entry {
        print(f"{visitor} is checking me out");
    }
}

edge Friend {}
edge Family {
    can announce with FriendFinder entry {
        print(f"{visitor} is traveling to family member");
    }
}

with entry {
    # Build the graph
    p1 = Person(name="John");
    p2 = Person(name="Susan");
    p3 = Person(name="Mike");
    p4 = Person(name="Alice");
    root ++> p1;
    p1 +>: Friend :+> p2;
    p2 +>: Family :+> [p1, p3];
    p2 +>: Friend :+> p3;
}

walker FriendFinder {
    has started: bool = False;

    can report_friend with Person entry {
        if self.started {
            print(f"{here.name} is a friend of friend, or family");
        } else {
            self.started = True;
            visit [-->];
        }
        visit [edge ->:Family :->];
    }

    can move_to_person with Root entry {
        visit [-->];
    }
}

with entry {
    result = FriendFinder() spawn root;
    print(result);
}
```

Now the first Person hits the `else`, flips `started` to True and expands via `visit [-->]`, and only the deeper nodes get the friend-of-friend label. The dropped level comes back.

I built a tiny graph with Alice connected to root and Bob connected to Alice, and my Greeter walker is supposed to print a hello for each person. But running it prints absolutely nothing.

```jac
node Person {
    has name: str;
    has age: int;
}

edge Knows {
    has since: int;
}

walker Greeter {
    can greet with Root entry {
        visit [<--];
    }

    can say_hello with Person entry {
        print(f"Hello, {here.name}!");
        visit [-->];
    }
}

with entry {
    # Build graph
    alice = Person(name="Alice", age=30);
    bob = Person(name="Bob", age=25);

    root ++> alice;
    alice +>: Knows(since=2020) :+> bob;

