Swapped the sequential loop for `asyncio.gather`, so all the independent `process_item` calls run concurrently and total time is roughly the slowest single item instead of the sum:

```jac
import asyncio;

walker async_processor {
    has items: list[object];

    async can process with Root entry {
        # Kick off every item at once; gather preserves input order.
        tasks = [process_item(item) for item in self.items];
        results = await asyncio.gather(*tasks);
        report list(results);
    }
}
```

`gather` keeps the results in the same order as `items`, so nothing downstream has to change -- you just stop waiting on each call serially.

cluster-wide debounce, review:

```jac
def maybe_run_periodic_task(task_id: str) -> bool {
    payload = {'pod': os.environ.get('HOSTNAME', 'local'), 'ts': time.time()};
    if _kv.set_nx_with_ttl(f'task_dbnce:{task_id}', payload, ttl=60) {
        run_task(task_id);
        return True;
    }
    return False;  # Another pod already ran it within the last 60s.
}
```

Added a try/except that deletes the debounce key if `run_task` fails, so a transient error frees the slot for the next tick instead of suppressing the task for a full minute:

```jac
import os;
import time;

def maybe_run_periodic_task(task_id: str) -> bool {
    key = f'task_dbnce:{task_id}';
    payload = {'pod': os.environ.get('HOSTNAME', 'local'), 'ts': time.time()};

    if not _kv.set_nx_with_ttl(key, payload, ttl=60) {
        return False;  # Another pod already ran it within the last 60s.
    }

    try {
        run_task(task_id);
        return True;
    } except Exception as e {
        # Roll back the claim so the next tick can retry instead of being
        # debounced away by a marker for work that never completed.
        _kv.delete_if_equals(key, payload);
        raise;
    }
}
```

Now a successful run holds the debounce for the intended 60s, but a failed run releases the claim immediately so the task isn't silently starved.

