        if arg == "--benchmark" or arg == "-b" {
            _benchmark_mode = 10;
        }
    }
    if _benchmark_mode > 0 {
        game.benchmark(_benchmark_mode);
    } else {
        game.play();
    }
}
```

It works, but the argument loop is written in a C-style index pattern that isn't idiomatic Jac:

```jac
for i in range(1, len(_args)) {
    arg = _args[i];
    ...
}
```

You copy `sys.argv` into `_args`, then manually index it just to skip element 0. Jac supports slicing, so `for arg in sys.argv[1:]` expresses "every arg after the program name" directly -- no index variable, no `len()`, no separate `arg = _args[i]` line. Fewer moving parts, and no way to get the off-by-one wrong.

Minor: `_benchmark_mode: int = 0` is a fine local, but the leading underscore plus explicit annotation on a plain flag counter is a bit heavy; `benchmark_mode = 0` reads cleaner. And note this file won't compile standalone since `Game` isn't defined here.

